#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096594 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0072fd14-c312-5229-9ffa-9b46c1ecd180" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Regression Line Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "15c6c8c2-9c56-5eff-bfc6-36cfe174ee3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9715" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $4$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nConsider the data sets above. You are given that $\\overline{x}=4$ and $\\overline{y}=3$. Find the equation of the corresponding linear regression line. Round the slope and the $y$-intercept to $1$ decimal place if necessary." :question/worked-solution "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny = mx+b\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}, b = y^(―) - mx^(―)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m{:}$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n$S_{xy}|= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(y_{i} - y^(―)); = (1 - 4)(6 - 3) + (2 - 4)(4 - 3); = + (4 - 4)(2 - 3) + (9 - 4)(0 - 3); = (-3)(3) + (-2)(1) + (0)(-1) + (5)(-3); =-9 - 2 - 0 - 15; =-26$\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n$S_{xx}|= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2}; = (1 - 4)^{2} + (2 - 4)^{2} + (4 - 4)^{2} + (9 - 4)^{2}; = 9 + 4 + 0 + 25; = 38$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m$, we obtain\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{-26}{38} = -\\dfrac{13}{19} \\approx -0.7\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb &= y^(―) - mx^(―) \\\\\n&= 3 - (-\\frac{13}{19}) \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= \\frac{109}{19} \\\\\n&\\approx 5.7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny = -0.7x + 5.7\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096595 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f904b9b7-b565-5bee-8f6f-c319a7cb9d7b" :knowledge-point/title "Making Predictions by Calculating a Regression Line"}] :question/id #uuid "9c9247a2-0a43-5dec-b477-bb60f8f57c0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9716" :question/problem "| **Years ($x$)** | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Value ($y$)** | **$35$** | **$26$** | **$19$** | **$16$** |\n\nThe table above gives the value of a car a few years after it was purchased (in thousands of dollars). You are given that $\\overline{x}=2$ and $\\overline{y}=24$. Find the equation of the corresponding linear regression line and use this to predict how long it takes for the car's value to drop to $\\[math]10\\,000$. Round your answer to the nearest year." :question/worked-solution "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny = mx+b\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}, b = y^(―) - mx^(―)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m{:}$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n$S_{xy}|= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(y_{i} - y^(―)); = (0 - 2)(35 - 24) + (1 - 2)(26 - 24); = + (3 - 2)(19 - 24) + (4 - 2)(16 - 24); = (-2)(11) + (-1)(2) + (1)(-5) + (2)(-8); =-22 - 2 - 5 - 16; =-45$\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n$S_{xx}|= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2}; = (0 - 2)^{2} + (1 - 2)^{2} + (3 - 2)^{2} + (4 - 2)^{2}; = 4 + 1 + 1 + 4; = 10$\n- Now, substituting into the formula for $m$, we obtain\n$m =-\\dfrac{45}{10} = -4.5$.\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb &= y^(―) - mx^(―) \\\\\n&= 24 - (-\\frac{9}{2}) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny = -4.5x+33\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate the required value by substituting $y=10$ into the equation of the trend line and solving for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -4.5x + 33 \\\\\n10 &= -4.5x + 33 \\\\\n- 23 &= -4.5x \\\\\n&= x \\mid \\approx 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, it takes approximately $5$ years for the car's value to drop to $\\[math]10\\,000$.\n\n**Note:** Since we're extrapolating (the point where $y=10$ is not within the original data range), our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f904b9b7-b565-5bee-8f6f-c319a7cb9d7b" :knowledge-point/title "Making Predictions by Calculating a Regression Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbe49d01-75e5-5b23-96a3-7651b1f296b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "107.7cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094442} {:answer/id #uuid "e947b64f-641f-5d6c-a8fc-d2c00c29bf46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "108.5cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094443} {:answer/id #uuid "07b7ca52-d1a1-5564-805b-6b82c293ffe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "108.6cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094444} {:answer/id #uuid "1213977b-5955-5c1a-8293-76d0870d40c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "107.9cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094445} {:answer/id #uuid "1d94ba4e-3a18-57e8-9f5f-ae6fac2e2225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "109.0cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094446}] :answer-field/id #uuid "67250215-1e03-538c-9563-cca87464372b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094441}] :question/id #uuid "c8d3e7fb-7b7e-5708-a3c3-52c0d9f411c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-134208" :question/problem "| **Age ($x$)** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Height ($y$)** | $95$ | $106$ | $110$ | $125$ |\n\nThe table above gives the ages, in years, and the corresponding heights, in centimeters, of Milly and Rasheed's four children. You are given that $x^(―) = 6$ and $y^(―) = 109$.\n\nFind the equation of the corresponding linear regression line and use this to predict the children's height when they are $6$ years old. Round the regression line's coefficients to three decimal places and your final answer to the nearest tenth of a centimeter.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
